/unruly
# The tool's FORMER name. Renaming the tool renamed this rule with it, which
# left a 16MB build of the old binary suddenly un-ignored -- and it was swept
# into "rename the tool to unruly" and has shipped in every clone since.
# Kept so the artifact cannot come back, and as the concrete reason this
# repository does not use `git add -A`: the rule that would have caught the
# file had just been renamed out from under it.
/supabasemap
# A file literally named "-u", 124 bytes of scanner output. It arrived in
# 6f9508c ("test(docs): every command in the documentation must parse") beside
# a test that only PARSES commands and never runs one -- so it was not that
# test's doing. It was a stray from a hand-run scan swept in by a
# non-explicit add, exactly as the 16MB supabasemap binary above was.
#
# Two accidental files from the same cause is why `git add -A` is not used
# here. A leading dash also makes it awkward to remove by hand, which is how
# it survived: `rm -u` is not a command, it is a flag.
/-u
/dist/
*.out
*.jsonl
fixtures/lab/supabase/.temp/
fixtures/lab/supabase/.branches/
# Fixture credentials. Pattern-based on purpose: this was previously a list of
# individual paths, and every fixture added after it was written slipped
# through — three .jwt files reached the repository that way. A rule that must
# be extended for each new file fails open, which is the wrong direction for a
# rule about credentials.
*.jwt
.secrets/
fixtures/supabase-lab/app/build/
cover.out
# Anchored, for the same reason as audit-report.md below: as a bare pattern
# this matched cmd/coveraudit/ as well as the compiled binary, so the SOURCE
# of the coverage auditor was absent from every clone -- and `make
# audit-offline`, the command a fork is told to run, failed on it.
/coveraudit
# Anchored at the repository root ON PURPOSE. As a bare pattern this matched
# at any depth, so docs/audit-report.md -- the report docs/auditing.md tells
# auditors to read -- was silently untracked and absent from every clone.
/audit-report.md
audit-logs/
fixtures/lab/site/
fixtures/lab/preview/
fixtures/lab/archive/
dist/

# Build artifacts. The exploitcheck binary was tracked for months: 9 MB of
# compiled output rewritten on every build, which is how a commit claiming to
# add an audit report actually contained a rebuilt binary and nothing else.
/exploitcheck
/unruly

# Third-party scan data. ~100 MB of harvested prospect URLs, per-site reports
# and study runs, sitting inside the working tree because the study was run
# from here. It is a separate git repository, which is the only reason a
# `git add -A` has not already pulled it in -- git skips nested repos rather
# than refusing. Remove that inner .git and the next add commits other
# people's scan results into an OSS scanner. Named explicitly so the outcome
# does not depend on that accident.
/supabase-prospects/
.DS_Store

# Batch-scan output. Two independent reasons, either one sufficient:
#
# Findings are proof-carrying by design, so a run directory holds real rows
# copied out of whatever was scanned. And run.json carries the local fixture's
# anon JWT -- a throwaway minted for 127.0.0.1, but the CI secret scan matches
# on shape and cannot know that. It reached a commit once via `git add -A`, in
# the same commit that added the rule above.
scan/runs/
benchmark-runner

# PocketBase fixture runtime state: the binary is fetched by setup.sh and the
# instances are provisioned from it. Nothing here is source.
fixtures/pocketbase/bin/
fixtures/pocketbase/*_data/
fixtures/pocketbase/*_migrations/
fixtures/pocketbase/*.log
.mutate.lock

# Cloud Functions fixture dependencies: installed to deploy, never committed.
node_modules/

# Python bytecode, from importing scripts/mutate.py in a helper.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
/estate
experiments/rls-agents/runs/

# Everything not for publication lives under one ignored directory.
#
# ONE line, and deliberately generic. .gitignore is itself published, so an
# entry naming `shodan-prospects.csv` or `firebase-lab/` would disclose the
# thing it exists to hide. A single opaque prefix discloses nothing and is the
# only rule a reviewer has to check.
/private/
